{#-
  Bifrost declarative configuration (config.json), rendered by playbooks/ai-hosts.yml
  --tags bifrost through the role's templates interface and bind-mounted read-only
  at $APP_DIR/config.json. JSON carries no comments, so everything explanatory in
  this file lives in Jinja comment blocks and never reaches the host.

  ansible_managed is deliberately absent for the same reason: a leading comment
  line would make the file invalid JSON.

  SECRETS NEVER APPEAR HERE. Bifrost resolves any string of the form
  "env.<NAME>" against the process environment at startup, and the environment is
  supplied by the 0600/no_log bifrost.env rendered alongside this file. This
  template is world-readable on the host by design so `--diff` shows what changed.

  CONFIG SOURCE MODEL. config_store is left at its default (enabled, SQLite in
  $APP_DIR), which is Bifrost's "DB-backed config.json" mode: the sections present
  here seed or reconcile the config store at startup under the default
  source_of_truth "split" — unchanged file-backed rows keep dashboard edits, while
  rows that changed in this file are applied on the next start. The role restarts
  the unit whenever this render changes, so an edit here reaches the gateway in
  the same play run. Set "config_store": {"enabled": false} for strict file-only
  operation instead; that also disables config edits from the dashboard and API.
-#}
{#-
  $schema is pinned to the SAME TAG as the running image, not to
  https://www.getbifrost.ai/schema. That live URL publishes the 2.x config
  surface, and validating this file against it is what put a `setup_token` key
  here that v1.6.10 has no concept of. Editor tooling should describe the binary
  actually running.

  Even the tag's own schema is slightly ahead of the Go structs (it documents
  governance.auth_config.disable_auth_on_inference, which is NOT a field on
  v1.6.10's AuthConfig and is silently dropped). Where schema and struct disagree,
  the struct wins — v1.6.10 decodes this file with a plain json.Unmarshal and no
  DisallowUnknownFields, so an unknown key is discarded without a warning of any
  kind. Nothing here may be trusted because a schema accepted it.
-#}
{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maximhq/bifrost/transports/{{ BIFROST_SERVICE_IMAGES.bifrost.tag }}/transports/config.schema.json",
  "encryption_key": "env.BIFROST_ENCRYPTION_KEY",
{#
  NO setup_token KEY. v1.6.10 has no setup-token mechanism at all: the string
  appears nowhere in its config structs, its loader, or its environment handling —
  it is a 2.x addition that only the live schema documents. Rendering it here did
  nothing except imply a bootstrap step that does not exist. The first admin
  account on this version comes from governance.auth_config below, which is
  reconciled into the config store on every start.

  Inference-call authentication. THE SWITCH IS HERE, ON client, and nowhere else:
  v1.6.10 reads ClientConfig.EnforceAuthOnInference (json enforce_auth_on_inference)
  and its built-in default is false. The similarly named
  governance.auth_config.disable_auth_on_inference that the schema and the
  upstream withauth example both show is NOT decoded by this version — setting it
  changes nothing and reports nothing. See the Internal config-bifrost.yml for the
  reachability tradeoff behind the shipped value.
#}
  "client": {
    "drop_excess_requests": {{ BIFROST_DROP_EXCESS_REQUESTS | bool | to_json }},
    "enable_logging": {{ BIFROST_ENABLE_LOGGING | bool | to_json }},
    "enforce_auth_on_inference": {{ BIFROST_ENFORCE_AUTH_ON_INFERENCE | bool | to_json }}
  },
{% if BIFROST_AUTH_ENABLED | bool -%}
{#-
  Dashboard and admin-API authentication. governance.auth_config is the current
  home for this block; the identically shaped top-level auth_config is deprecated
  but still decoded. Both credentials are env references resolved from bifrost.env.

  EXACTLY THREE FIELDS ARE DECODED by v1.6.10's AuthConfig struct — is_enabled,
  admin_username, admin_password. Do not add disable_auth_on_inference here even
  though the tag's schema and upstream's own examples/configs/withauth/config.json
  both show it: this version has no such field and drops it silently. Inference
  auth is client.enforce_auth_on_inference, set above.

  FAIL-CLOSED ON UNRESOLVED REFERENCES. If BIFROST_ADMIN_USERNAME or
  BIFROST_ADMIN_PASSWORD is missing from the environment, the loader logs
  "username/password set with external reference but value is empty" and refuses
  to persist empty credentials. Those two strings are the first thing to grep for
  in the container log if the dashboard ever comes up open.

  Turning auth off later means setting BIFROST_AUTH_ENABLED to false, which drops
  this block from the file. The config store keeps its own enabled row, so a
  dashboard that stays locked after that has to be corrected from the dashboard's
  Security tab, not from here.

  VERIFYING IT WORKED does not require a browser, and a browser is the least
  reliable way to check it — a session cookie from an earlier visit suppresses the
  login prompt without any config being wrong. Ask the gateway instead:

    curl -s http://<host>:<port>/api/session/is-auth-enabled
    -> {"auth_type":"password","has_valid_token":false,"is_auth_enabled":true}

  That endpoint is deliberately public (it is on the middleware's whitelist) and
  is the same source of truth the dashboard itself uses to decide whether to
  prompt. is_auth_enabled true plus a 401 from any /api/ route means this block is
  in force.
#}
  "governance": {
    "auth_config": {
      "is_enabled": true,
      "admin_username": "env.BIFROST_ADMIN_USERNAME",
      "admin_password": "env.BIFROST_ADMIN_PASSWORD"
    }
  },
{% endif -%}
{#-
  Provider pool. `vllm` is a first-class provider type in the current schema, so
  no custom_provider_config is needed: the endpoint and the exact served model
  name are carried per key in vllm_key_config, and the provider's network_config
  intentionally has no base_url field for that reason. The key is keyless — vLLM
  serves no API key of its own — so base_key.value is omitted.

  models ["*"] lets any requested model name route to this key; the request is
  then served by whatever vllm_key_config.model_name names, which is the single
  model this instance serves. Narrow it to that one name if the gateway later
  fronts more than one backend.

  FALLBACK SHAPE, proven by the retired in-cluster llama.cpp deployment, if this
  native provider ever misbehaves against a GGUF-serving vLLM: declare a custom
  provider instead — "custom_provider_config": {"base_provider_type": "openai",
  "is_key_less": true, "allowed_requests": {"chat_completion": true,
  "chat_completion_stream": true}} with "network_config": {"base_url": "<url>"}
  and a plain key. Request paths are relative to base_url and the openai base
  type already defaults to /v1/chat/completions, which is what vLLM serves.
#}
  "providers": {
    "vllm": {
      "keys": [
        {
          "name": {{ BIFROST_VLLM_KEY_NAME | to_json }},
          "models": ["*"],
          "weight": 1.0,
          "vllm_key_config": {
            "url": {{ BIFROST_VLLM_URL | to_json }},
            "model_name": {{ BIFROST_VLLM_MODEL_NAME | to_json }}
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
